  <!-- SECTION 3: WHERE IT FALLS SHORT -->
  <div class="section" id="friction">
    <div class="section-header">
      <span class="section-num">03 — Where Onboarding Falls Short</span>
      <h2 class="section-heading">The drop-off after the kickoff call is the main failure mode.</h2>
      <p class="section-tagline">Lower scores cluster around a consistent experience: the kickoff call was good, but customers felt unsupported or underprepared once it ended. Technical gaps — SCIM, AI features — amplify this. Customers who hit a blocker after the call had no obvious next step.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <div class="accent-block c3-border">
          <div class="block-title">Post-kickoff drop-off</div>
          <div class="block-body">The most common pattern in 7-8/10 responses: the kickoff was described positively, but implementation afterward felt entirely self-directed. Customers used phrases like "on my own," "hurried," and "not covered." The call ends and there is no clear second step for things that didn't get resolved.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="accent-block c3-border">
          <div class="block-title">SCIM and IdP integration failures</div>
          <div class="block-body">Multiple customers cite SCIM as a specific blocker. Okta SCIM import failing is named directly. EntraAD SCIM not being supported causes delays. These failures happen after the call and customers don't always know who to contact or what the timeline to resolution looks like. One customer waited "a long time" for a support response and specifically called out the absence of 24/7 support.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="accent-block c3-border">
          <div class="block-title">AI features feel gated behind the team</div>
          <div class="block-body">Customers who were sold on deepfakes and AI capabilities discovered that using them in practice requires submitting a request to the Adaptive team. This mismatch between what was shown in the demo and what they could do on day one created friction. For a few, it downgraded the onboarding score even when everything else went smoothly.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <div class="accent-block c3-border">
          <div class="block-title">Use cases beyond the basics weren't covered</div>
          <div class="block-body">Several customers noted the kickoff covered phishing and training well but didn't address their specific setup: shared mailboxes, unique IdP configurations, compliance requirements, non-standard user structures. These customers felt they had to discover gaps on their own and then find support.</div>
